<!-- Synthetic teaching example. Review and load on a disposable Wazuh 4.x manager.
     Check IDs are unused. These rules have not been validated against a live engine.

     Written after PHP's 21 October 2026 emergency release for CVE-2026-109618 and
     CVE-2026-109619 (nicknamed "PHP2Shell"), two unauthenticated RCE bugs in PHP
     core affecting PHP-FPM, mod_php, php-cgi, FrankenPHP and LiteSpeed. Fixed in
     8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35. This file contains no exploit code: it is
     post-compromise indicator patterns to adapt to your own decoders and log
     sources, then prove out with `ruleguard run` before trusting them.

     Confidence of each base rule, checked against the Wazuh 4.x rules syntax
     reference and FIM docs on 2026-10-22:
       - 554 ("File added to the system") is a documented default FIM rule and is
         used as written below.
       - There is no single documented default rule for "a shell was spawned by
         this parent process" across every Wazuh install: that depends entirely
         on which exec-monitoring log source you run (auditd execve rules,
         Sysmon for Linux, an EDR agent's own decoder). Rule 100211 below has no
         <if_sid>: it is a standalone rule matching directly on field names
         (parent_process, process) that are placeholders, not a verified
         decoder's real output. Give it a real <if_sid> or <if_group> scoped to
         your own exec-monitoring decoder before you load it, or it can fire on
         anything that happens to carry fields with these names.
       - There is likewise no single documented default rule for "this host just
         logged a php-fpm crash line" independent of your syslog decoder. Rule
         100212 below is the same kind of standalone placeholder: no <if_sid>,
         generic field names (program_name, message) that must be checked
         against what your actual syslog decoder produces before you rely on it. -->
<group name="php2shell_indicators,">
  <!-- 1. A new PHP (or other executable) file appears inside a web root, written by
       the web server's own account. A legitimate deploy writes these as the deploy
       user, not as www-data/php-fpm/apache at request time, so this is a plausible
       webshell-drop signal. if_sid 554 is Wazuh's documented default FIM rule for
       a newly added file (enable alert_new_files in your syscheck config). -->
  <rule id="100210" level="12">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">\.(php|phtml|php5)$</field>
    <field name="uname_after" type="pcre2">^(www-data|php-fpm|apache|nginx)$</field>
    <description>Demo: new PHP file written to disk by the web server's own account.</description>
    <mitre><id>T1505.003</id></mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 2. A shell spawned with a web-server process as its parent. A worker handling
       plain PHP requests normally never forks sh/bash; after PHP2Shell-class RCE,
       the dropped shell runs as whatever user php-fpm/apache/nginx runs as.
       NOT VERIFIED against a default decoder: if_sid and the field names are
       placeholders for whatever exec-monitoring source you actually run. -->
  <rule id="100211" level="13">
    <field name="parent_process" type="pcre2">^(php-fpm|apache2|httpd|nginx)$</field>
    <field name="process" type="pcre2">^(sh|bash|dash|nc|curl|wget)$</field>
    <description>Demo: shell or outbound tool spawned by the web server process. Adapt if_sid and
      the field names to your actual exec-monitoring decoder (auditd, Sysmon for Linux, or an
      EDR agent); this has not been checked against any one of them.</description>
    <mitre><id>T1059.004</id></mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3. A burst of php-fpm worker crashes or out-of-memory kills in a short window.
       Both CVEs are memory-corruption bugs (OOM cleanup and use-after-free), so
       repeated crashes right before a webshell appears is a plausible precursor,
       not proof on its own, pair it with rules 100210/100211 above.
       100212 is the single-event base rule; 100213 is the frequency rule that
       counts repeats of 100212 specifically (never of its own id, that cannot
       fire and some managers reject it as circular). NOT VERIFIED: if_sid and
       field names are placeholders for your actual syslog decoder. -->
  <rule id="100212" level="5">
    <field name="program_name">^php-fpm$</field>
    <field name="message" type="pcre2">(segfault|SIGSEGV|out of memory|oom-killer)</field>
    <description>Demo: a single php-fpm crash or OOM kill line. Adapt if_sid and the field names
      to your actual syslog decoder; this has not been checked against any one of them.</description>
  </rule>
  <rule id="100213" level="8" frequency="5" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100212</if_matched_sid>
    <description>Demo: 5 or more php-fpm crashes or OOM kills (rule 100212) within 2 minutes.</description>
  </rule>
</group>
